  "worked_solution": "The power set of $T,$ denoted $P(T),$ is the set that contains every possible subset of $T,$ including the empty set $∅.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that $2$ is not a set. So, the notation $P(P(2))$ does not make sense.\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that we are given the set $\\{2\\}.$ So, its power set must be $\\{∅,\\{2\\}\\}.$ Therefore, $P(P(\\{2\\}))=\\{∅,\\{∅\\},\\{\\{2\\}\\},\\{∅,\\{2\\}\\}\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that we are given the set $\\{\\{2\\}\\}.$ So, its power set must be $\\{∅,\\{\\{2\\}\\}\\}.$ Therefore, $P(P(\\{\\{2\\}\\}))=\\{∅,\\{∅\\},\\{\\{\\{2\\}\\}\\},\\{∅,\\{\\{2\\}\\}\\}\\}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
